Task 1: Understanding Static vs Dynamic Inventory
Subtask 1.1: Review a Static Inventory

Static inventories are fixed text files that must be manually updated whenever infrastructure changes. Dynamic inventories query a live source (a cloud API, a database, a CMDB) at run time and always reflect current state.

    Create a working directory and a sample static inventory:

mkdir -p ~/lab11
cd ~/lab11
cat > static_inventory << 'EOF'
[webservers]
web1.example.com ansible_host=10.0.1.10
web2.example.com ansible_host=10.0.1.11

[databases]
db1.example.com ansible_host=10.0.2.10

[production:children]
webservers
databases
EOF

    List the static inventory:

ansible-inventory -i static_inventory --list

Deliverable / expected result: The command prints a JSON structure containing webservers, databases, and production groups, each listing the hardcoded hostnames above. This confirms you understand the baseline format that dynamic inventories replace.
Subtask 1.2: Verify AWS Access for Dynamic Inventory

    Check AWS CLI and credentials:

aws --version
aws configure list

    Confirm boto3 is importable:

python3 -c "import boto3; print('boto3 version:', boto3.__version__)"

Deliverable / expected result: aws --version prints a version string (for example aws-cli/2.x), and the Python check prints boto3 version: <x.y.z> with no ImportError. If aws configure list shows no access key, note this - Task 2 will still work but will return an empty host list.
Task 2: Configuring and Testing the AWS Dynamic Inventory
Subtask 2.1: Create the Dynamic Inventory Configuration

    Create the inventory directory:

mkdir -p ~/lab11/inventory
cd ~/lab11

    Create the aws_ec2 plugin configuration file. The file name must end in aws_ec2.yml or aws_ec2.yaml so Ansible recognizes it as this plugin's config.

cat > inventory/aws_ec2.yml << 'EOF'
---
plugin: amazon.aws.aws_ec2
regions:
  - us-east-1
filters:
  instance-state-name: running
keyed_groups:
  - prefix: tag
    key: tags
  - prefix: instance_type
    key: instance_type
  - prefix: az
    key: placement.availability_zone
groups:
  webservers: "'web' in (tags.Name | default(''))"
  databases: "'db' in (tags.Name | default(''))"
compose:
  ansible_host: public_ip_address | default(private_ip_address)
  ec2_state: instance_state_name
  ec2_instance_type: instance_type
  ec2_az: placement.availability_zone
EOF

    Validate the YAML and plugin recognition:

ansible-inventory -i inventory/aws_ec2.yml --list

Deliverable / expected result: The command returns valid JSON. If AWS credentials are configured and running EC2 instances exist in us-east-1, you will see a _meta.hostvars section populated with instance data. If no instances exist (or no credentials are configured), you will see an empty or near-empty JSON structure like {"_meta": {"hostvars": {}}, "all": {"children": ["ungrouped"]}} with no error about "unable to parse" - this is a valid, working configuration correctly reporting zero matching hosts.
Subtask 2.2: View Inventory as a Group Tree

ansible-inventory -i inventory/aws_ec2.yml --graph

Deliverable / expected result: A tree view starting with @all: and showing child groups such as @webservers, @databases, @ungrouped, and any tag_*, instance_type_*, or az_* groups generated by keyed_groups. If zero instances were discovered, the tree still prints with empty group branches - this confirms the plugin loaded and evaluated correctly.
Task 3: Running Playbooks Against a Dynamic Inventory
Subtask 3.1: Gather and Review System Information

    Create the playbooks directory:

mkdir -p ~/lab11/playbooks

    Create the system information playbook:

cat > ~/lab11/playbooks/gather_system_info.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Gather System Information from Dynamic Inventory
  hosts: all
  gather_facts: yes
  vars:
    output_dir: "/tmp/system_reports"

  tasks:
    - name: Create output directory
      file:
        path: "{{ output_dir }}"
        state: directory
        mode: '0755'
      delegate_to: localhost
      run_once: true

    - name: Display basic system information
      debug:
        msg: |
          Host: {{ inventory_hostname }}
          OS: {{ ansible_distribution | default('N/A') }} {{ ansible_distribution_version | default('') }}
          IP Address: {{ ansible_default_ipv4.address | default('N/A') }}

    - name: Create system report
      copy:
        content: |
          System Report for {{ inventory_hostname }}
          =====================================
          Hostname: {{ ansible_hostname | default('N/A') }}
          Operating System: {{ ansible_distribution | default('N/A') }} {{ ansible_distribution_version | default('') }}
          Instance Type: {{ ec2_instance_type | default('N/A') }}
          Availability Zone: {{ ec2_az | default('N/A') }}
          State: {{ ec2_state | default('N/A') }}
        dest: "{{ output_dir }}/{{ inventory_hostname }}_report.txt"
      delegate_to: localhost
EOF

    Run the playbook against the dynamic inventory:

cd ~/lab11
ansible-playbook -i inventory/aws_ec2.yml playbooks/gather_system_info.yml

Deliverable / expected result: Ansible connects to each discovered host (or reports PLAY RECAP with zero hosts processed if none were discovered) and, for each host it reaches, writes a report file. If you have no live EC2 instances, add a local test target so you can verify the playbook logic end to end:

ansible-playbook -i localhost, -c local playbooks/gather_system_info.yml

    Review the generated reports:

ls -l /tmp/system_reports/
cat /tmp/system_reports/*.txt

Deliverable / expected result: At least one file named <hostname>_report.txt exists in /tmp/system_reports/, and cat displays its contents showing the hostname, OS fields, and EC2 metadata (or N/A for fields that do not apply to a local test host).
Subtask 3.2: Create a Group-Specific Playbook

    Create a web server check playbook that targets only the webservers group:

cat > ~/lab11/playbooks/check_webservers.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Check Web Servers from Dynamic Inventory
  hosts: webservers
  gather_facts: no

  tasks:
    - name: Report group membership and EC2 metadata
      debug:
        msg: |
          Host {{ inventory_hostname }} is in groups: {{ group_names | join(', ') }}
          Instance Type: {{ ec2_instance_type | default('N/A') }}
          Availability Zone: {{ ec2_az | default('N/A') }}
EOF

    Run it in check mode against the dynamic inventory:

ansible-playbook -i inventory/aws_ec2.yml playbooks/check_webservers.yml --check

Deliverable / expected result: If your AWS account has instances tagged with Name containing web, the play runs against them and prints their group membership and EC2 metadata. If the webservers group is empty, Ansible reports skipping: no hosts matched for the play, which is the correct and expected behavior for an empty dynamic group.
Subtask 3.3: Build and Run a Combined Deployment Playbook

    Create a playbook that imports both playbooks in sequence and adds a verification step:

cat > ~/lab11/playbooks/full_deployment.yml << 'EOF'
---
- import_playbook: gather_system_info.yml
- import_playbook: check_webservers.yml

- name: Post-run verification
  hosts: all
  gather_facts: no

  tasks:
    - name: Confirm report file exists for this host
      stat:
        path: "/tmp/system_reports/{{ inventory_hostname }}_report.txt"
      register: report_file
      delegate_to: localhost

    - name: Display verification result
      debug:
        msg: >-
          Report for {{ inventory_hostname }} exists: {{ report_file.stat.exists }}
EOF

    Run the full deployment playbook and observe each phase execute in order:

ansible-playbook -i inventory/aws_ec2.yml playbooks/full_deployment.yml

Deliverable / expected result: The output shows three distinct PLAY sections in sequence (system info gathering, web server check, post-run verification), each with its own PLAY RECAP. In the final play, the Display verification result task prints Report for <hostname> exists: True for every host that was processed in the first play. If you are testing without live EC2 hosts, run the same command against a local target to confirm the full workflow logic:

ansible-playbook -i localhost, -c local playbooks/full_deployment.yml

Task 4: Troubleshooting Dynamic Inventory Configurations
Subtask 4.1: Create a Diagnostic Script

    Create the troubleshooting script:

cat > ~/lab11/troubleshoot_inventory.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

echo "Dynamic Inventory Troubleshooting Script"
echo "========================================"
echo

echo "1. Checking AWS CLI Configuration:"
echo "   AWS CLI Version: $(aws --version 2>&1)"
echo "   AWS Region: $(aws configure get region 2>/dev/null || echo 'Not configured')"
echo

echo "2. Checking AWS Credentials:"
if aws sts get-caller-identity >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo "   OK: AWS credentials are valid"
    aws sts get-caller-identity --query 'Account' --output text | sed 's/^/   Account: /'
else
    echo "   FAIL: AWS credentials are invalid or not configured"
fi
echo

echo "3. Checking Python Dependencies:"
python3 -c "import boto3; print('   OK: boto3 version', boto3.__version__)" 2>/dev/null || echo "   FAIL: boto3 not installed"
python3 -c "import botocore; print('   OK: botocore version', botocore.__version__)" 2>/dev/null || echo "   FAIL: botocore not installed"
echo

echo "4. Checking Ansible Configuration:"
echo "   Ansible Version: $(ansible --version | head -1)"
plugin_count=$(ansible-doc -t inventory -l 2>/dev/null | grep -c aws_ec2)
echo "   aws_ec2 plugin registered: ${plugin_count} match(es) found"
echo

echo "5. Testing Inventory File:"
if [ -f "inventory/aws_ec2.yml" ]; then
    echo "   Testing inventory/aws_ec2.yml..."
    if ansible-inventory -i inventory/aws_ec2.yml --list >/tmp/inv_test_output.json 2>/tmp/inv_test_error.log; then
        echo "   OK: inventory parsed successfully"
        host_count=$(python3 -c "import json; d=json.load(open('/tmp/inv_test_output.json')); print(len(d.get('_meta', {}).get('hostvars', {})))")
        echo "   Hosts discovered: ${host_count}"
    else
        echo "   FAIL: inventory parsing failed"
        echo "   Error details:"
        sed 's/^/     /' /tmp/inv_test_error.log
    fi
else
    echo "   FAIL: inventory/aws_ec2.yml not found in current directory"
fi
echo

echo "6. Checking EC2 Instance Visibility:"
if aws ec2 describe-instances --query 'Reservations[*].Instances[*].InstanceId' --output text >/tmp/ec2_ids.txt 2>/tmp/ec2_error.log; then
    total=$(wc -w < /tmp/ec2_ids.txt)
    running=$(aws ec2 describe-instances --filters "Name=instance-state-name,Values=running" --query 'Reservations[*].Instances[*].InstanceId' --output text | wc -w)
    echo "   OK: can query EC2 instances"
    echo "   Total instances visible: ${total}"
    echo "   Running instances: ${running}"
else
    echo "   FAIL: cannot query EC2 instances"
    sed 's/^/     /' /tmp/ec2_error.log
fi
echo

echo "Troubleshooting complete."
echo "If issues persist, check:"
echo "- AWS IAM permissions (ec2:DescribeInstances read access required)"
echo "- That the inventory file name ends in aws_ec2.yml or aws_ec2.yaml"
echo "- Network connectivity from the control node to AWS endpoints"
echo "- Collection installed: ansible-galaxy collection install amazon.aws"
EOF

    Make it executable and run it:

chmod +x ~/lab11/troubleshoot_inventory.sh
cd ~/lab11
./troubleshoot_inventory.sh

Deliverable / expected result: The script prints six numbered diagnostic sections, each ending in an OK: or FAIL: line. Review your output line by line: every FAIL: line identifies exactly one configuration gap (missing credentials, missing package, wrong file name, or missing IAM permission) that you can resolve before rerunning Task 2 and Task 3.
Subtask 4.2: Apply the Script to Diagnose an Intentional Misconfiguration

    Deliberately break the inventory file name (a common real-world mistake) and observe the effect:

cp inventory/aws_ec2.yml inventory/aws_ec2_broken.txt
./troubleshoot_inventory.sh

Deliverable / expected result: Section 5 of the script output still reports on inventory/aws_ec2.yml (unaffected), demonstrating that the script checks a specific expected filename. Now manually confirm the broken file is invisible to Ansible:

ansible-inventory -i inventory/aws_ec2_broken.txt --list

This produces an error similar to Unable to parse .../aws_ec2_broken.txt as an inventory source, because the plugin is matched by file suffix (aws_ec2.yml / aws_ec2.yaml). Clean up afterward:

rm inventory/aws_ec2_broken.txt

This exercise demonstrates the single most common dynamic inventory failure mode: a correctly written plugin configuration that Ansible silently ignores because the file name does not match the plugin's required suffix pattern.
Challenge Exercise (Optional)

Apply what you have learned to a new scenario not covered above:

    Create a new dynamic inventory file inventory/aws_ec2_challenge.yml that only discovers instances tagged Environment: dev (hint: use the filters key with tag:Environment as the filter name, or a groups entry with a Jinja2 condition on tags.Environment).
    Add a keyed_groups entry that groups hosts by tags.Project instead of by tags.Name.
    Run ansible-inventory -i inventory/aws_ec2_challenge.yml --graph and confirm the group structure reflects your new grouping logic.
    Run ./troubleshoot_inventory.sh against this new file by temporarily copying it over inventory/aws_ec2.yml, and confirm section 5 reports it as successfully parsed.

There is no single correct file for this exercise; success is measured by whether your configuration parses without error and produces the grouping behavior you intended.
Expected Outcomes

    A working aws_ec2.yml dynamic inventory configuration that parses successfully and reflects live (or empty) AWS EC2 state
    Executed playbooks that consume dynamic inventory group membership and per-host EC2 metadata, with generated report files reviewed in /tmp/system_reports
    A reusable troubleshooting script that diagnoses AWS credentials, Python dependencies, plugin registration, file naming, and IAM permission issues

Troubleshooting

    Symptom: ansible-inventory -i inventory/aws_ec2.yml --list returns an "Unable to parse" or "no inventory was parsed" error. Diagnostic hint: Check that the file name ends exactly in aws_ec2.yml or aws_ec2.yaml, and that the plugin: key value is exactly amazon.aws.aws_ec2; run ansible-doc -t inventory -l | grep aws_ec2 to confirm the collection is installed.

    Symptom: The inventory parses but returns zero hosts even though EC2 instances exist in the console. Diagnostic hint: Run aws sts get-caller-identity to confirm which account/credentials Ansible is using, verify the regions: list in the config matches where your instances actually run, and check that filters: instance-state-name: running is not excluding stopped instances you expected to see.

Conclusion

In this lab you configured and tested an AWS aws_ec2 dynamic inventory, compared it against a traditional static inventory, and ran playbooks that used dynamically discovered hosts and their EC2 metadata to generate and verify system reports. You built a multi-play deployment workflow with import_playbook and confirmed each phase executed and produced verifiable output, and you used a diagnostic script to systematically troubleshoot credential, dependency, and configuration-naming problems, including a deliberately broken file name to see a real failure mode firsthand. These skills translate directly to real-world automation work, where infrastructure changes constantly and static host lists quickly become stale or incorrect. As a next step, extend this lab by connecting the same inventory plugin to a second cloud provider's equivalent plugin (such as azure.azcollection.azure_rm or google.cloud.gcp_compute), or by integrating the generated system reports into a monitoring or configuration-management pipeline.
